      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "Persistent pain is a common consequence of spinal cord injury. A patient-specific assessment that combines both the identification of pain symptoms and psychosocial factors is needed for a tailored treatment approach. The aim of the study was to define pain symptom profiles and to determine their relationship with psychosocial factors in persons with spinal cord injury."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Face-to-face interview and examination."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "VA Medical Center and Miami Project to Cure Paralysis, Miami, Florida."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENTS",
          "content": "Persons with spinal cord injury (135 men and 21 women) provided detailed descriptions of 330 neuropathic pains."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "OUTCOME MEASURES",
          "content": "The American Spinal Injury Impairment Scale, pain history and measures of pain interference, life satisfaction, locus of control, social support and depression."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The exploratory factor analyses and regression analyses revealed three distinct symptom profiles: 1) aching, throbbing pain, aggravated by cold weather and constipation predicted by a combination of chance locus of control and lower levels of life satisfaction; 2) stabbing, penetrating, and constant pain of high intensity predicted by a combination of pain interference, localized pain, powerful others locus of control and depressed mood; and 3) burning, electric, and stinging pain aggravated by touch and muscle spasms predicted by pain interference."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Although these results need to be replicated in other spinal cord injury samples, our findings suggest that pain symptom profiles may be a useful way to further characterize pain in a comprehensive assessment strategy."
        }
      ]},
